//? Title: CSV Features: Fit Once on Training Rows
//? Subtitle: Missing values and unseen categories need different output columns
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A mixed numeric and categorical table must become a fixed-width matrix without using held-out
information. We fit moments and a vocabulary on three training rows, then transform missing,
unseen and extreme held-out values. Quoted fields and header reordering test the parser and
schema independently of any prediction model.
)
\toc

> The method

Parsing and feature fitting solve different problems. CSV quoting allows delimiters and newlines
inside a cell; doubled quotes represent literal quotes. ParseCsv preserves exact strings and
requires unique nonempty headers and rectangular records. FeatureColumns then interprets selected
numeric cells using finite classic-locale numbers, treating trimmed empty cells as missing.
For observed training values, population moments and the transformation are

$$
\mu=\frac1m\sum_i x_i,\quad s^2=\frac1m\sum_i(x_i-\mu)^2,\qquad z(x)=\frac{x-\mu}{s}.
$$
\alttext(Compute moments on observed training values only, then standardise every later observed value with those fixed moments.)

Numeric columns emit z and a missing indicator. Missing z is zero (mean imputation); constant
or entirely missing columns use s=1, with mean zero for entirely missing columns. Categorical
columns emit separate missing and unknown buckets before a sorted training vocabulary. This
makes missing different from a new nonempty string. Column lookup uses names, so reordering
is harmless. Fitting stores no held-out values. A dense output needs O(rows times encoded width)
space; large vocabularies can make dense one-hot encoding expensive.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/csv_features_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/csv_features_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/csv_features_concept.tikz > build/csv_features_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/csv_features_concept.svg build/csv_features_concept.dvi > build/csv_features_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/csv_features_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
